WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.240
- What we need to see
is inflation coming down

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:05.270
in a clear and convincing way.

00:00:05.270 --> 00:00:07.810
- I think that bringing inflation down

00:00:07.810 --> 00:00:10.410
should be our number one priority.

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:12.950
- [Narrator] The story
of inflation is changing.

00:00:12.950 --> 00:00:15.190
- A year ago, Wall Street,

00:00:15.190 --> 00:00:17.440
a lot of policy makers in Washington

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:20.220
were hoping that the
inflation uptick would be

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:21.710
what they called transitory.

00:00:21.710 --> 00:00:23.540
- There was a decent amount of evidence

00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:25.290
to support that view.

00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:28.130
That it would be temporary or transitory.

00:00:28.130 --> 00:00:29.870
- That it would come and go away.

00:00:29.870 --> 00:00:32.060
Well, that's not what happened.

00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:34.030
- [Narrator] To understand
where inflation is spreading.

00:00:34.030 --> 00:00:36.420
In the average American's
household budget,

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:39.180
we look to the Labor
Department's inflation metric,

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:41.080
the consumer price index.

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:44.020
We came away with some
surprises and some lessons

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:46.910
to tell us how the story
of inflation is changing.

00:00:46.910 --> 00:00:48.860
The inflation we're currently experiencing

00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:51.650
began as pandemic lockdown
restrictions were lifted,

00:00:51.650 --> 00:00:53.420
triggering a boom in demand.

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:56.220
- When inflation turned up in 2021,

00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:57.870
it was a different kind of inflation

00:00:57.870 --> 00:00:59.340
than what we're seeing right now.

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:00.900
There were really two facets to it.

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:03.420
One was we saw these
idiosyncratic changes.

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:05.700
For instance, used car prices jumped up

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:07.890
in part because there were
shortages of new cars.

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:10.270
- [Narrator] Supply chain
bottlenecks and chip shortages

00:01:10.270 --> 00:01:12.530
pushed up prices for goods like cars,

00:01:12.530 --> 00:01:13.797
both new and old.

00:01:13.797 --> 00:01:15.620
- And then the other thing that we saw

00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:18.890
was prices for other goods jumping up.

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:21.820
During the pandemic,
people binged on goods.

00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:23.130
They couldn't go out to restaurants.

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:24.470
They couldn't go to hotels.

00:01:24.470 --> 00:01:26.380
And so they order in Amazon

00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:29.040
and the spending picked
up on a lot of goods.

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:32.440
And then as the pandemic
receded a little bit,

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:35.060
we had these supply shortages

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:36.740
for imported goods in particular.

00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:39.590
So you saw things like
furniture prices go up,

00:01:39.590 --> 00:01:41.780
apparel prices, shoe prices.

00:01:41.780 --> 00:01:44.140
Stuff that used to be really cheap

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:47.420
because we were importing it
from low cost labor places

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:48.960
was all of a sudden popping up.

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:49.793
(light music)

00:01:49.793 --> 00:01:52.210
- [Narrator] But the inflation
we've seen recently in 2022

00:01:52.210 --> 00:01:54.840
is more severe and more wide reaching.

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:56.850
- Now we're seeing prices for services,

00:01:56.850 --> 00:01:59.200
like one of my favorite
indicators is haircuts.

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:01.170
For 20 years, the price of a haircut

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:04.440
was going up between zero and 4%.

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:07.350
In this latest stage, they've gone up 5%

00:02:07.350 --> 00:02:10.000
because the cost of labor is going up.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:12.350
- [Narrator] Some of the most
noticeable recent price hikes

00:02:12.350 --> 00:02:14.010
have been at the grocery store.

00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:17.620
13% of the average American's
household budget goes to food.

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:19.910
And since last April,
household food prices

00:02:19.910 --> 00:02:22.510
are up almost 11%.

00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:25.900
- Grocery prices were pretty
tame actually last year.

00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:27.570
And what we've seen this year

00:02:27.570 --> 00:02:30.380
is increases at double digit levels.

00:02:30.380 --> 00:02:31.520
- [Narrator] Since last year,

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:34.960
prices for ground beef
have risen almost 15%

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:38.670
and prices for chicken
breasts are up 16.4%.

00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:40.570
- So meat is a very interesting case.

00:02:40.570 --> 00:02:44.670
When you're building a herd this year,

00:02:44.670 --> 00:02:47.380
you're building it for
two or three years out.

00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:48.910
You have to have some view

00:02:48.910 --> 00:02:51.280
of what the world is gonna
look like in a couple years.

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:53.320
Cows don't grow overnight, right?

00:02:53.320 --> 00:02:56.810
In 2020, when some of these
herds were being built,

00:02:56.810 --> 00:02:59.250
we were going into a
devastating recession.

00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:00.440
Now, two years later,

00:03:00.440 --> 00:03:03.230
we're dealing with meat shortages

00:03:03.230 --> 00:03:05.500
because there's this undersupply of meat.

00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:07.680
- [Narrator] Meanwhile,
a combination of factors

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:09.582
caused gas prices to soar.

00:03:09.582 --> 00:03:11.040
(tank wheels revving)

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:13.550
The Russia's invasion of
Ukraine earlier this year

00:03:13.550 --> 00:03:15.040
jolted the global market,

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:17.110
prompting oil price increases.

00:03:17.110 --> 00:03:18.790
- Russia's invasion of Ukraine

00:03:18.790 --> 00:03:21.170
has taken things like gasoline prices

00:03:21.170 --> 00:03:24.350
from a stage where they were
recovering from the pandemic

00:03:24.350 --> 00:03:26.840
to where they're much higher
than they were before.

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:28.790
- [Narrator] In late May, gasoline prices

00:03:28.790 --> 00:03:31.680
topped $4 a gallon in all 50 states

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:32.960
for the first time ever.

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:34.950
Currently, California leads the country

00:03:34.950 --> 00:03:36.303
with prices over $6.

00:03:37.159 --> 00:03:39.000
- The war in Ukraine has had a big impact

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:41.210
on global energy markets.

00:03:41.210 --> 00:03:45.200
When COVID hit, we went
into this global downturn.

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:47.110
The price of gasoline in the U.S. went

00:03:47.110 --> 00:03:49.890
from around $3 a gallon to $2 a gallon.

00:03:49.890 --> 00:03:54.890
In 2021, by early '22, it was
back up to over $3 a gallon.

00:03:55.480 --> 00:03:57.000
So there was a recovery.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:03:58.670
We had been through a full cycle.

00:03:58.670 --> 00:04:01.100
Then Russia invaded Ukraine

00:04:01.100 --> 00:04:02.690
and now we have gasoline prices

00:04:02.690 --> 00:04:05.040
over $5 a gallon in many places.

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:06.250
- [Narrator] Higher energy prices

00:04:06.250 --> 00:04:08.250
have also bled into air travel.

00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:10.330
Analysts say that rising fuel costs

00:04:10.330 --> 00:04:11.870
and staffing shortages have led

00:04:11.870 --> 00:04:13.480
to a spike in airfares.

00:04:13.480 --> 00:04:17.710
In April, airline fairs surged
18.6% from a month earlier,

00:04:17.710 --> 00:04:19.730
the fastest rise on record.

00:04:19.730 --> 00:04:22.220
- Americans are really
frustrated right now

00:04:22.220 --> 00:04:23.180
with what's happening

00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:25.170
and one great place to see that

00:04:25.170 --> 00:04:27.050
is in what's happening at the airport.

00:04:27.050 --> 00:04:29.180
Flights are being
canceled at the same time

00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:30.620
that airfares are going up.

00:04:30.620 --> 00:04:31.640
- [Narrator] We also found

00:04:31.640 --> 00:04:34.490
that the biggest budget
item for most households,

00:04:34.490 --> 00:04:36.530
the cost of keeping a roof over your head,

00:04:36.530 --> 00:04:37.530
is heading up.

00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:40.530
Housing makes up 30% of the
average household's budget

00:04:40.530 --> 00:04:43.030
and it's up 5% from a year ago.

00:04:43.030 --> 00:04:46.230
- So rents are another facet
of this inflation problem.

00:04:46.230 --> 00:04:49.150
It relates to undersupply of housing

00:04:49.150 --> 00:04:52.210
that was built really
over the last 10 years

00:04:52.210 --> 00:04:54.750
since the 2008 financial crisis

00:04:54.750 --> 00:04:58.560
and led to less building
for over a decade.

00:04:58.560 --> 00:04:59.980
And now we're paying a price for that

00:04:59.980 --> 00:05:04.870
because we're seeing
price increases for homes

00:05:04.870 --> 00:05:07.860
and that's feeding through
into rental prices.

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:11.240
- We now are entering
a period of transition

00:05:11.240 --> 00:05:13.860
from one of historic recovery

00:05:13.860 --> 00:05:17.790
to one that can be marked
by stable and steady growth.

00:05:17.790 --> 00:05:20.440
Making this shift is a central piece

00:05:20.440 --> 00:05:24.630
of the President's plan to
get inflation under control

00:05:24.630 --> 00:05:28.110
without sacrificing the
economic gains we've made.

00:05:28.110 --> 00:05:29.090
- [Narrator] As the country heads

00:05:29.090 --> 00:05:30.560
into the midterm election season,

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:32.310
much of the political discussion

00:05:32.310 --> 00:05:35.520
has centered around the
damaging impact of inflation.

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:38.420
- I'm meeting with the Chairman
today and Secretary Yellen

00:05:39.630 --> 00:05:41.580
to discuss my top priority,

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:44.210
and that is addressing inflation.

00:05:44.210 --> 00:05:45.820
- The Fed has shifted its strategy

00:05:45.820 --> 00:05:46.830
over the last few months.

00:05:46.830 --> 00:05:48.540
What they're trying to do now,

00:05:48.540 --> 00:05:50.120
and what they think they have to do

00:05:50.120 --> 00:05:51.800
is hit the demand side of the economy.

00:05:51.800 --> 00:05:53.960
And they're gonna do that
by raising interest rates

00:05:53.960 --> 00:05:56.280
to make it more expensive to borrow.

00:05:56.280 --> 00:06:00.200
The Fed is trying to hit
demand to slow growth,

00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:02.240
economic growth, just enough

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:06.260
so that inflation comes down
to more manageable levels

00:06:06.260 --> 00:06:08.000
of around 2% a year.

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:12.040
So one risk is that they
don't hit demand enough

00:06:12.040 --> 00:06:14.110
and inflation remains elevated.

00:06:14.110 --> 00:06:15.770
The other thing that could go wrong

00:06:15.770 --> 00:06:18.650
is that they hit demand so hard

00:06:18.650 --> 00:06:21.560
that not only do they bring down inflation

00:06:21.560 --> 00:06:24.340
but they drive the whole
economy into recession.

00:06:24.340 --> 00:06:27.060
And in that scenario, the real risk

00:06:27.060 --> 00:06:29.200
that people need to worry about

00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:31.950
is that in the process of
going into a recession,

00:06:31.950 --> 00:06:33.550
they kill this great job market

00:06:33.550 --> 00:06:35.262
and unemployment starts rising.

00:06:35.262 --> 00:06:37.762
(light music)

